{"name":"Clean Canvas","hex":"#f6e9d3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"clean","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Free of dirt, filth, or impurities (extraneous matter); not dirty, filthy, or soiled.","Of metal, having relatively few impurities."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A removal of dirt.","The first part of the event clean and jerk in which the weight is brought from the ground to the shoulders."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clean"},{"term":"canvas","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of coarse cloth, woven from hemp (traditionally) or from cotton and polyesters, useful for making sails, tents, and overcoats or as a surface for paintings.","A piece of such cloth stretched across a frame on which one may paint an artwork."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover (an area or object) with canvas."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/canvas"}],"entries":[{"title":"Canvas","blocks":[{"text":"Canvas is a durable plain-woven fabric used for making sails, tents, marquees, backpacks, shelters, as a support for oil painting and for other items for which sturdiness is required, as well as in such fashion objects as handbags, electronic device cases, and shoes. It is popularly used by artists as a painting surface, typically stretched across a wooden frame."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Sj%C3%B6manss%C3%A4ck_-_1991.jpg/330px-Sj%C3%B6manss%C3%A4ck_-_1991.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canvas","wikidata":"Q4259259"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/MetroBrussel_Erasmus.jpg/500px-MetroBrussel_Erasmus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/MetroBrussel_Erasmus.jpg/500px-MetroBrussel_Erasmus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia